---

# Requirements

## General

BML requires:

- C++17-compatible compiler
- CMake 3.18 or newer
- Git

BML is written in C++ and does not require a separate runtime or scripting language.

## Linux

On Debian/Ubuntu, the basic build environment can be installed with:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential cmake ninja-build git
```

Both GCC and Clang can be used.

For example, to use Clang explicitly:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++
```

## Windows

A Microsoft Visual C++ installation with C++ development support is required.

The Visual Studio C++ workload must include:

- MSVC
- Windows SDK
- C++ build tools

CMake can generate a Visual Studio solution automatically:

```powershell
cmake -S . -B build
```

Visual Studio generators are multi-configuration generators. Therefore, `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is not used to select Debug or Release when building with Visual Studio.

---

# Repository Structure

The BML source tree is organised approximately as follows:

```text
BML/
├── CMakeLists.txt
├── include/
│   └── BML/
│       ├── ...
│       └── export.hpp
├── src/
│   ├── instantiations.cpp
│   ├── matrix.cpp
│   ├── testMatrix.cpp
│   └── ...
└── ...
```

Public headers are located below:

```text
include/BML/
```

The implementation is located below:

```text
src/
```

`src/instantiations.cpp` contains the library's explicit template instantiations.

---

# Configuring BML

A build directory should normally be kept separate from the source tree.

## Linux — Ninja

For a Release build:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
```

For a Debug build:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
```

## Windows — Visual Studio

Configure with:

```powershell
cmake -S . -B build
```

CMake will select the installed Visual Studio generator and compiler.

The resulting directory contains separate configurations such as:

```text
build/
├── Debug/
└── Release/
```

---

# Build Options

BML provides the following CMake options.

## BML_BUILD_SHARED

Controls which library the `BML` alias refers to.

Default:

```text
BML_BUILD_SHARED=ON
```

To select the shared library:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_BUILD_SHARED=ON
```

To select the static library:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_BUILD_SHARED=OFF
```

The underlying `BML_shared` and `BML_static` targets are available independently.

## BML_RUN_TESTS

Controls whether the tests are included in a normal build.

Default:

```text
BML_RUN_TESTS=ON
```

Enable:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_RUN_TESTS=ON
```

Disable:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_RUN_TESTS=OFF
```

When enabled, the normal build runs tests for both the shared and static libraries.

## BML_ENABLE_LTO

If available in the current CMake configuration, this option controls BML's LTO/IPO support.

Enable:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_ENABLE_LTO=ON
```

Disable:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -DBML_ENABLE_LTO=OFF
```

When LTO is enabled, BML checks whether the selected compiler supports CMake's interprocedural optimisation mechanism before enabling it. CMake's `CheckIPOSupported` module is specifically intended for this purpose and supports Visual Studio generators as well.

---

# Building BML

## Linux

After configuring:

```bash
cmake --build build
```

For a parallel build:

```bash
cmake --build build --parallel
```

## Windows

Debug:

```powershell
cmake --build build --config Debug
```

Release:

```powershell
cmake --build build --config Release
```

Parallel Release build:

```powershell
cmake --build build --config Release --parallel
```

---

# Running the Tests

BML contains functional and stress tests covering the supported matrix types and operations.

With:

```text
BML_RUN_TESTS=ON
```

the normal build includes both test targets.

A successful test run ends with:

```text
[BML TEST] All tests completed successfully.
```

## Linux

The test executables can also be run directly:

```bash
./build/testMatrix_shared
./build/testMatrix_static
```

## Windows

For a Release build:

```powershell
.\build\Release\testMatrix_shared.exe
.\build\Release\testMatrix_static.exe
```

For a Debug build:

```powershell
.\build\Debug\testMatrix_shared.exe
.\build\Debug\testMatrix_static.exe
```

Both the shared and static tests should pass before an installation is considered successful.

---

# Building Shared and Static Libraries

BML produces two actual library targets:

```text
BML_shared
BML_static
```

The `BML` target is an alias selecting one of these targets according to `BML_BUILD_SHARED`.

## Linux

The shared library is normally:

```text
libBML.so
```

The static library is:

```text
libBML.a
```

## Windows

The shared library consists of:

```text
BML.dll
BML.lib
```

`BML.dll` contains the actual shared-library implementation.

`BML.lib` is the MSVC import library used when linking an application against the DLL.

The static library is:

```text
BML_static.lib
```

The static library contains the object code required for static linking.

---

# LTO / IPO

BML can use interprocedural optimisation (IPO), commonly referred to as link-time optimisation (LTO).

The CMake configuration checks compiler support before enabling IPO on the relevant targets.

The configuration reports:

```text
Building with LTO
```

when LTO is enabled and supported.

LTO is compiler-dependent:

- GCC uses GCC LTO.
- Clang uses LLVM LTO.
- MSVC uses link-time code generation (LTCG).

LTO can make intermediate object files and static libraries considerably larger. The size of a static library is therefore not a reliable indication of the size of the final executable.

The final linked executable or shared library is where the optimisation is resolved.

---

# Installing BML

BML uses CMake's standard installation mechanism and `GNUInstallDirs`.

Install the configured build with:

```bash
sudo cmake --install build
```

The default installation prefix is normally:

```text
/usr/local
```

On a typical Linux installation this results in files under:

```text
/usr/local/include/BML/
```

and:

```text
/usr/local/lib/
```

The exact library directory is determined by CMake's installation-directory configuration.

Using relative installation destinations allows the installation prefix to be changed without hard-coding `/usr/local` into the project.

---

# Using an Installed BML

After installation, public BML headers can be included normally:

```cpp
#include <BML/Matrix.hpp>
```

The exact header names depend on the part of the BML API being used.

For a standard `/usr/local` installation, GCC can normally find the headers and libraries without additional include or library paths.

For a non-standard installation prefix, specify the appropriate paths.

For example:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 main.cpp \
    -I/path/to/prefix/include \
    -L/path/to/prefix/lib \
    -lBML \
    -o myprogram
```

---

# Static Linking

To link against the static library on Linux:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 main.cpp \
    -I/usr/local/include \
    -L/usr/local/lib \
    -lBML \
    -o myprogram
```

The static library contains the BML implementation directly in the resulting executable.

On Windows, link against:

```text
BML_static.lib
```

The resulting executable does not require `BML.dll` for BML itself.

---

# Shared Linking

On Linux:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 main.cpp \
    -I/usr/local/include \
    -L/usr/local/lib \
    -lBML \
    -o myprogram
```

If `/usr/local/lib` is not already known to the dynamic linker, update the linker cache:

```bash
sudo ldconfig
```

Alternatively, for temporary testing:

```bash
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
```

The resulting program requires:

```text
libBML.so
```

at runtime.

---

# Windows DLL Usage

A Windows application using the shared BML library requires:

```text
BML.dll
```

at runtime.

The application is linked against:

```text
BML.lib
```

A typical deployment therefore contains:

```text
myprogram.exe
BML.dll
```

The import library:

```text
BML.lib
```

is required when linking the application but normally does not need to be distributed with the finished application.

For static linking, use:

```text
BML_static.lib
```

and no BML DLL is required at runtime.

---

# Windows Symbol Exports

BML uses explicit symbol visibility control for shared-library builds.

Public API declarations use:

```cpp
BML_API
```

The export mechanism is defined in:

```text
include/BML/export.hpp
```

When building the Windows DLL, CMake defines:

```text
BML_BUILDING_DLL
```

which causes public symbols to be exported with:

```cpp
__declspec(dllexport)
```

Consumers of the DLL use:

```text
BML_USE_DLL
```

which causes the declarations to use:

```cpp
__declspec(dllimport)
```

This explicit export mechanism is necessary for Windows DLLs.

Public classes, functions and operators that form part of the shared-library ABI must therefore be marked appropriately with `BML_API`.

---

# Inspecting the Windows DLL

Visual Studio provides `dumpbin` for inspecting the generated binaries.

To list exported DLL symbols:

```powershell
dumpbin /exports BML.dll
```

To search for particular BML symbols:

```powershell
dumpbin /exports BML.dll |
    Select-String "Matrix|BoolRef|RowView|StringStorage"
```

To inspect the static library:

```powershell
dumpbin /linkermember:2 BML_static.lib
```

To inspect the import library:

```powershell
dumpbin /linkermember:2 BML.lib
```

---

# Custom Installation Prefix

The installation prefix can be changed during configuration.

For example:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
```

Build and install:

```bash
cmake --build build
cmake --install build
```

The resulting installation will be placed below:

```text
$HOME/.local/
```

This allows BML to be installed without root privileges.

Another common choice is:

```text
/opt/BML
```

For example:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/BML
```

Then:

```bash
sudo cmake --install build
```

---

# Cleaning the Build

To completely remove generated build files:

## Linux

```bash
rm -rf build
```

## Windows PowerShell

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force build
```

A clean configuration can then be created again:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build
```

A clean build is recommended after changing fundamental compiler or CMake configuration.

---

# Recommended Release Builds

## Linux

For a clean Release build with Ninja:

```bash
rm -rf build

cmake -S . -B build \
    -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DBML_BUILD_SHARED=ON \
    -DBML_RUN_TESTS=ON \
    -DBML_ENABLE_LTO=ON

cmake --build build --parallel
```

Run the tests:

```bash
./build/testMatrix_shared
./build/testMatrix_static
```

Then install:

```bash
sudo cmake --install build
```

## Windows

For a clean Visual Studio Release build:

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force build

cmake -S . -B build `
    -DBML_BUILD_SHARED=ON `
    -DBML_RUN_TESTS=ON `
    -DBML_ENABLE_LTO=ON

cmake --build build --config Release --parallel
```

Run the tests:

```powershell
.\build\Release\testMatrix_shared.exe
.\build\Release\testMatrix_static.exe
```

Then inspect the generated DLL if required:

```powershell
dumpbin /exports .\build\Release\BML.dll
```

---

# Build Output

A successful Windows Release build is expected to contain files similar to:

```text
build/
└── Release/
    ├── BML.dll
    ├── BML.exp
    ├── BML.lib
    ├── BML_static.lib
    ├── testMatrix_shared.exe
    └── testMatrix_static.exe
```

A Linux build normally produces:

```text
build/
├── libBML.so
├── libBML.a
├── testMatrix_shared
└── testMatrix_static
```

The exact generated files can vary slightly between generators and platforms.

---

# Troubleshooting

## CMake cannot find a compiler

Verify that the compiler is installed and available:

```bash
g++ --version
```

or:

```bash
clang++ --version
```

On Windows, configure from a Visual Studio Developer PowerShell or use a Visual Studio generator supported by the installed CMake.

## LTO is not available

BML checks compiler support before enabling IPO/LTO.

If the compiler does not support the required IPO configuration, CMake reports the reason and does not enable it.

LTO is an optimisation feature and is not required for BML to function correctly.

## Windows DLL links but symbols are missing

Check the DLL's export table:

```powershell
dumpbin /exports BML.dll
```

Public API classes/functions that need to cross the DLL boundary must be declared with:

```cpp
BML_API
```

Check that the shared-library target is being built with:

```text
BML_BUILDING_DLL
```

and that the application receives:

```text
BML_USE_DLL
```

## Tests fail after changing the build configuration

Perform a clean build:

```bash
rm -rf build
```

or in PowerShell:

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force build
```

Then configure and build again.

---

# Summary

A standard Linux Release build is:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build --parallel
```

A standard Windows Release build is:

```powershell
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --config Release --parallel
```

Run both shared and static tests before installation.

Install with:

```bash
sudo cmake --install build
```

BML supports both:

```text
Shared:
    Linux:   libBML.so
    Windows: BML.dll + BML.lib

Static:
    Linux:   libBML.a
    Windows: BML_static.lib
```

The same BML source tree is therefore usable for both static and shared builds across the supported platforms.